{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. He inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his young age a regency was established led by his mother and later by his uncle Baldwin III of Jerusalem. During this time the County of Tripoli was under constant threat from Muslim forces especially from Nur ad-Din Zengi and later Saladin. Raymond III was known for his cautious approach to warfare and preferred diplomacy over open conflict.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was married to Eschiva of Bures and later to Stephanie of Milly. His marriage alliances strengthened his position in the region. He became regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond III was appointed regent in 1174 and served until 1177. His regency was controversial as he was often accused of being too passive and unwilling to confront Saladin. Despite this he managed to maintain peace with some Muslim leaders and avoided major battles. He was also involved in the internal power struggles between the nobility of the kingdom and the military orders like the Templars and Hospitallers.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nIn 1182 Raymond III was briefly deposed as regent by Baldwin IV who favored his brother-in-law Guy of Lusignan. Raymond was exiled to his county but returned to power after Baldwin IVs death in 1185. He once again became regent for the young Baldwin V. Raymond III died in 1187 shortly before the catastrophic Battle of Hattin. His death removed a moderate voice from the Crusader leadership and contributed to the rise of more aggressive factions. The fall of Jerusalem followed soon after. Raymond III is remembered as a cautious and politically astute leader who tried to preserve the Crusader states through negotiation rather than confrontation. His legacy is complex as he was both criticized for indecisiveness and praised for his efforts to avoid unnecessary bloodshed. He left behind a county that was still strong but a kingdom that was on the brink of collapse.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "log